"""HEADLINE BUG found by m12_btree: `str != str` always returns true.

Repro (one screenful):

    a: str = "hello"
    b: str = "hello"
    println(str(a == b))    # prints: true   (correct)
    println(str(a != b))    # prints: true   (WRONG — should be false)

Root cause (compiler/src/ir.rs ≈ line 1716):

    AstBinOp::Eq => {
        if is_str { IROp::StrEq } else if is_float { IROp::FEq } else { IROp::IEq }
    }
    AstBinOp::Ne => if is_float { IROp::FNe } else { IROp::INe },
                                                   ^^^^^^^^^^^^
                                                   no is_str branch!

`StrEq` was added to IROp (line 92), but there is no `StrNe`. When
the lowerer sees `str != str` it falls through to `INe`, which
compares the two string-handle pointers as integers. Since every
string literal materialises a fresh handle, distinct equal strings
have distinct pointers → `INe` returns true.

Symptoms in the wild:
- m12_btree.spy: ALL search-and-compare-result tests printed FAIL
  even when the searched value was correct (`got = one` then
  `got != "one" → true`).
- Any program using `s != ""` as a "missing" sentinel will
  effectively go through the "missing" branch every time.
- Programs that work today by accident: any code that uses
  `if a == b` (positive form) instead of `!=` is unaffected.

Fix sketch: add `IROp::StrNe`, mirror StrEq's VM dispatch with a
negated result. Or as a smaller change, lower `AstBinOp::Ne` on
str operands as `StrEq` followed by `BoolNot`. Either is a few
lines in `compiler/src/ir.rs`.

Workaround used in m12_btree.spy: `not (a == b)` everywhere.
"""

fn main() -> i32:
    a: str = "hello"
    b: str = "hello"
    c: str = "world"

    println("a == b → " + str(a == b))   # expect true   — gets true   (OK)
    println("a != b → " + str(a != b))   # expect false  — gets true   (BUG)
    println("a == c → " + str(a == c))   # expect false  — gets false  (OK)
    println("a != c → " + str(a != c))   # expect true   — gets true   (OK)

    # Workaround: `not (a == b)`.
    println("not (a == b) → " + str(not (a == b)))  # expect false — gets false (OK)
    return 0
